    "markdown": "First assessment 2025. Connect models of structure with explanations of reactivity. This compact map omits fine-grained understandings: shared topics contain level-specific content, so check the linked guide before declaring an advanced treatment SL.\n\n# Structure\n## S1. Particle models\nS1.1 Matter and particles; S1.2 nuclei/atoms; S1.3 electron configurations; S1.4 moles; S1.5 ideal gases.\n## S2. Bonding and materials\nS2.1 ionic; S2.2 covalent; S2.3 metallic; S2.4 models applied to materials.\n## S3. Classification\nS3.1 periodicity; S3.2 organic functional groups.\n\n# Reactivity\n## R1. Energetics\nR1.1 measured enthalpy changes; R1.2 energy cycles; R1.3 fuels.\n### R1.4 Entropy and spontaneity (HL)\n## R2. Amount, rate and equilibrium\nR2.1 reaction quantities; R2.2 kinetics; R2.3 extent of reaction.\n## R3. Reaction mechanisms\nR3.1 proton transfer; R3.2 electron transfer; R3.3 electron sharing; R3.4 electron-pair sharing. Not all detail in these topics is shared by SL and HL.\n\n# Assessment\nPaper 1: 36%; paper 2: 44%; scientific investigation: 20%, maximum 3,000-word report. Practise experimental interpretation alongside calculations. Distinguish observations, models and assumptions.\n## Examinations (SL)\nPaper 1: 90 minutes; paper 2: 90 minutes.\n## Examinations (HL)\nPaper 1: 120 minutes; paper 2: 150 minutes. Additional depth occurs throughout structure and reactivity, beyond R1.4.\n\n# Sources\n[IB Chemistry guide](https://www.ibo.org/globalassets/new-structure/university-admission/pdfs/subject-guides/chemistry-guide.pdf)\n[IB subject brief, 2025](https://www.ibo.org/globalassets/new-structure/recognition/pdfs/dp_sciences_chemistry_subject-brief_jan_2022_e.pdf)"
