    "markdown": "For examinations from 2027. The 2014 course ending in 2026 is different: do not reuse its options or three-paper assessment. This reference outlines topics rather than every learning statement.\n\n# A. Computing concepts\n## A.1 Computer fundamentals\n## A.2 Networks\n## A.3 Databases\n## A.4 Machine learning\n\n# B. Computational problem-solving\n## B.1 Computational thinking\n## B.2 Programming\n## B.3 Object-oriented programming\nPython or Java is used in this course. Establish the class's language before generating assessed-style code.\n## B.4 Abstract data types (HL)\n\n# Case study\nBoth levels have case-study study time; depth and examination treatment differ. Retrieve the relevant session's issued material rather than inventing its contents.\n\n# Assessment\nThe computational-solution IA and a collaborative sciences project form the practical programme. Keep requirements, implementation, testing and evaluation connected when giving project feedback.\n## Papers and IA (SL)\nPaper 1: 75 minutes, 35%; paper 2: 75 minutes, 35%; IA: 30%.\n## Papers and IA (HL)\nPaper 1: 120 minutes, 40%; paper 2: 120 minutes, 40%; IA: 20%. HL extends shared topics as well as adding B.4.\n\n# Sources\n[IB course page and transition](https://www.ibo.org/programmes/diploma-programme/curriculum/sciences/computer-science/)\n[IB subject brief, 2027](https://www.ibo.org/globalassets/new-structure/university-admission/pdfs/dp_comp_sci_subjectbrief_en.pdf)"
