    "markdown": "First assessment 2022. Interpret models using evidence and their assumptions. The nine concepts are scarcity, choice, efficiency, equity, well-being, sustainability, change, interdependence and intervention. This map does not enumerate each level-specific understanding.\n\n# 1. Foundations\nEconomic questions, methods and the problem of resource allocation.\n# 2. Microeconomics\nDemand/supply, equilibrium, elasticity, intervention and market failures, including externalities, public goods and common resources.\n## Additional microeconomics (HL)\nAsymmetric information, market power and firms' market structures; further quantitative depth.\n# 3. Macroeconomics\nNational activity, aggregate demand/supply, objectives and policies, distribution, demand management and supply-side approaches.\n# 4. Global economy\nTrade, protection, integration, exchange rates, balance of payments, sustainable development and development strategies.\n## Additional global-economy work (HL)\nTerms of trade and further quantitative/policy analysis; confirm the guide's precise level boundaries.\n\n# Assessment\nIA: three news-based commentaries, maximum 800 words each, using different units and different key concepts. Apply diagrams and evidence to the article; do not merely summarize it. Check article eligibility and dates against the current guide.\n## Papers and IA (SL)\nPaper 1: 75 minutes, 30%; paper 2: 105 minutes, 40%; IA: 30%.\n## Papers and IA (HL)\nPaper 1: 75 minutes, 20%; paper 2: 105 minutes, 30%; paper 3: 105 minutes, 30%; IA: 20%. Paper 3 includes policy recommendations.\n\n# Sources\n[IB SL brief](https://www.ibo.org/globalassets/new-structure/programmes/dp/pdfs/sl-economics-en.pdf)\n[IB HL brief](https://www.ibo.org/globalassets/new-structure/programmes/dp/pdfs/hl-economics-en.pdf)"
