    "markdown": "DP core course, without SL/HL. Ask how knowledge is produced, justified and limited. This is a compact reference; session-specific essay titles must be retrieved separately.\n\n# Core theme\nKnowledge and the knower: examine your assumptions and communities of knowers.\n# Optional themes\nStudy two: knowledge and technology, language, politics, religion or indigenous societies. Establish which themes the class chose.\n# Areas of knowledge\nHistory, human sciences, natural sciences, arts and mathematics. Compare methods and standards of evidence without reducing an area to a stereotype.\n# Knowledge questions\nConnect claims to concrete examples, examine contrasting perspectives and explain implications. Keep discussion about knowledge rather than drifting into a general subject essay.\n\n# Assessment\n## Exhibition\nThree objects tied to one prescribed prompt; combined commentary maximum 950 words; 33% of TOK. Make each object's particular context matter to the justification.\n## Essay\nMaximum 1,600 words on one of six session-specific prescribed titles; 67%. Use the exact title issued for the student's examination session. Do not substitute a remembered or invented title.\n\n# Sources\n[IB TOK overview](https://www.ibo.org/programmes/diploma-programme/curriculum/dp-core/theory-of-knowledge/)\n[IB subject brief, 2022](https://www.ibo.org/globalassets/new-structure/programmes/dp/pdfs/tok-sb-english.pdf)"
